# Copy to .env and fill in. NEVER commit .env.
# Backend-only secrets: these must never reach the Chrome extension, the repo, or logs.

# Nebius Token Factory API key — https://docs.tokenfactory.nebius.com/quickstart
# Create it in the Token Factory console: https://tokenfactory.nebius.com/
NEBIUS_API_KEY=

# Web search for the general agent loop (Step 9). Optional: without a key the loop
# still answers, and says which parts it could not check. Set EITHER one - Exa wins
# if both are present. Both are read server-side only; neither reaches the extension.
#
# Exa - free tier includes monthly credits, no card: https://dashboard.exa.ai/api-keys
# EXA_API_KEY=
# Tavily - alternative provider: https://app.tavily.com/home
TAVILY_API_KEY=

# --- Google Calendar, read-only (Step 12) ---
#
# Optional. Without a client ID the brief simply has no calendar section, and
# nothing is requested from Google at all.
#
# 1. https://console.cloud.google.com -> create (or pick) a project
# 2. "APIs & Services" -> "Library" -> enable **Google Calendar API**
# 3. "APIs & Services" -> "OAuth consent screen" -> External, then add yourself
#    under "Test users" (an unverified app in testing mode only works for the
#    accounts listed there - this is the step people miss)
# 4. "Credentials" -> "Create credentials" -> "OAuth client ID" ->
#    application type **Desktop app**
# 5. Copy the client ID here. The secret is optional for a desktop client; put it
#    in GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET if your client issued one.
#
# Then start the backend and press "Connect Google Calendar" in the brief.
# The consent screen asks for one permission, "see the events on your calendars",
# and the backend can never change an event - that is the only scope requested.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Where the refresh token is kept. Default is data/google-token.json, which is
# gitignored, written 0600, and never leaves this machine. Only override this to
# point at a different file (the tests do, to use a temp one).
# CADENCE_GOOGLE_TOKEN=

# --- The voice: ElevenLabs, optional (Step 13) ---
#
# Without this the panel speaks with Chrome's built-in voice: free, local,
# robotic, and perfectly usable. With it, replies are synthesised on the backend
# and played back by the panel, so the key stays here and never reaches the
# extension bundle.
#
# Two things to know before you turn it on:
#
#   * It costs money per character. Replies are capped at 1,500 characters and
#     repeated replies come from a small in-memory cache, so "Speak again" is
#     free. Nothing is written to disk.
#   * The reply text is sent to ElevenLabs. A reply can quote what you told
#     Cadence ("your organic chemistry midterm is on November 12"), so this is a
#     change in who sees what. Chrome's voice keeps it on your machine.
#
# Get a key at https://elevenlabs.io -> your profile -> API keys. The free tier is
# enough to try this.
ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=

# Optional: which voice, and which model. Defaults are Adam and the low-latency
# Flash model. ElevenLabs' own docs use Rachel, but Rachel is a *library* voice
# now and a free-plan key is refused those with 402 paid_plan_required; the
# premade voices (Adam pNInz6obpgDQGcFmaJgB, Sarah, Daniel, George) are not.
# ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID=pNInz6obpgDQGcFmaJgB
# ELEVENLABS_MODEL_ID=eleven_flash_v2_5

# --- The public demo (Step 10) ---
#
# The demo never uses the ElevenLabs key even when one is set: a stranger's reply
# would be billed to the operator. `/health` reports `voice.available: false` there
# and the panel uses Chrome's voice instead.
#
# CADENCE_DEMO=on serves the built panel as a plain page at /demo, on a
# throwaway copy of demo/'s sample course load, with a request cap per visitor
# (max_requests_per_session_demo in config/models.json). Build the panel first:
#
#   cd extension && npm install && npm run build
#   CADENCE_DEMO=on python3 backend/server.py   # then open 127.0.0.1:8787/demo
#
# It is deliberately incompatible with CADENCE_TOKEN and the server refuses to
# start if both are set: a public demo cannot require a token, and a token-gated
# server must not serve the panel to strangers. Running with this on means the
# API is open for as long as the process is up - the cap bounds casual use, not
# an attacker with a different IP.
CADENCE_DEMO=
